<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- Run as a platform binary rather than a sandboxed app: gh has to read and
         write the user's repositories anywhere on the filesystem, and to fork
         and exec git. -->
    <key>platform-application</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.private.security.no-sandbox</key>
    <true/>
    <key>get-task-allow</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- Deliberately absent, and worth saying why, because the sibling project
         (CCForiOS) needs all of them and this one needs none:

         * JIT (dynamic-codesigning, com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit,
           allow-unsigned-executable-memory, skip-library-validation)
           Claude Code is a Bun binary and JavaScriptCore cannot start without a
           working JIT. gh is Go: ahead-of-time compiled, nothing in the process
           ever writes to executable memory. iOS's W^X enforcement is simply not
           in the picture.

         * Keychain (application-identifier, keychain-access-groups)
           gh stores its token through zalando/go-keyring, which on darwin shells
           out to /usr/bin/security -- a binary iOS does not have. That fails
           fast with ENOENT and gh falls back to its plaintext config file, a
           path it supports on purpose (it is what Linux without a secret service
           does). No entitlement can conjure up the missing CLI, so there is
           nothing to grant. If this ever grows a direct SecItem backend, the two
           keys go back here; CCForiOS confirmed SecItemAdd works on iOS with a
           keychain group named after the package. -->
</dict>
</plist>
